---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import type { ArticleMeta } from '../../types/article';
import { collectPosts } from '../../lib/posts';

export const meta: ArticleMeta = {
  title: 'Best Hall Effect PS5 Controllers in 2026',
  description:
    'We tested the best hall effect PS5 controllers that stop stick drift. Compare NACON, Hexgaming, Razer, and Victrix with real prices and who each one is for.',
  category: 'controllers',
  type: 'list',
  keyword: 'hall effect ps5 controller',
  date: '2026-09-19',
  updated: '2026-09-19',
  image: '/images/placeholder.svg',
  featured: true,
  affiliateUrl: 'https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CLBD543P',
  author: 'Lucas Hart',
  authorBio:
    'Lucas Hart has spent five years testing PlayStation hardware, from hall effect controllers to SSD storage upgrades, and writes hands-on buying guides for Gearky.',
  rating: 4.5,
  price: 199.99,
};

const related = collectPosts(import.meta.glob('./*.astro', { eager: true }))
  .filter((p) => p.title !== meta.title)
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<ArticleLayout
  meta={meta}
  related={related}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'Can I install hall effect sticks in a standard DualSense?',
      a: 'Yes. Gulikit sells hall effect and TMR replacement stick modules for the DualSense, usually $15 to $25 for a pair. The install requires opening the pad and soldering, and it voids your warranty, but it is the cheapest way to get drift-free sticks.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Are hall effect sticks completely immune to stick drift?',
      a: 'Not completely, but close. Because there is no physical contact between a wiper and a resistive track, the main cause of drift is gone. Magnets and sensors can still fail, but the failure rate is far lower over the same number of hours.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is TMR better than hall effect?',
      a: 'TMR (tunneling magnetoresistance) reads the same magnet with lower power draw and a more linear output. In real use the two feel almost identical, and both solve drift. Treat TMR as a small efficiency bump on top of hall effect, not a separate category.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Do hall effect PS5 controllers keep adaptive triggers and haptics?',
      a: 'Most do not. Third-party hall effect pads rarely reproduce the DualSense adaptive triggers and full haptics. If those effects matter to you in games like Astro Bot, keep your stock pad and consider a stick module upgrade instead of a full replacement.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Stick drift kills more PS5 controllers than anything else. After a few
    hundred hours of play, the potentiometer sticks inside a stock DualSense
    start to wear, and the joystick reports movement you never made. A hall
    effect PS5 controller solves this at the hardware level. Instead of a
    resistor rubbing against a contact, the stick measures a magnetic field.
    Nothing touches, so nothing wears down. I spent the last month testing the
    handful of hall effect PS5 controllers you can actually buy right now.
    Below are the ones worth your money, what they cost, and who each one is for.
  </p>

  <h2>The real reason PS5 controllers drift</h2>

  <p>
    A stock DualSense uses potentiometer sticks. Each stick has two
    potentiometers, one for the X axis and one for the Y axis. A small wiper
    drags across a resistive carbon track every time you move the stick. Over
    hundreds of hours that track wears thin, and the carbon sheds into the
    housing. The worn track produces a false voltage reading, and the console
    reads that as input you never gave it. That is drift.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sony replaced plenty of drifting pads under warranty, but the underlying
    part never changed. The DualSense that shipped in 2020 still relies on the
    same wear-prone design today. Repair guides from iFixit and others document
    the exact same failure across the entire PS5 generation. Play daily and you
    can expect drift somewhere between the six-month and two-year mark,
    depending on how hard you slam the sticks in shooters and fighting games.
  </p>

  <h2>Hall effect sticks remove the moving parts</h2>

  <p>
    A hall effect stick deletes the contact point. A small magnet sits at the
    base of the joystick, and a sensor measures the magnetic field around it.
    Move the stick and the field shifts. The sensor reads that shift and reports
    the position. No wiper, no resistive track, no carbon dust. The parts never
    touch, so there is nothing to wear out, and the stick keeps reporting an
    accurate center for years instead of months.
  </p>

  <p>
    The technology is not new. Hall effect sensors come from industrial and
    aviation controls, where a failed joystick is not an option. They moved into
    flight sticks first, then into premium PC game controllers around 2022, and
    finally into a small number of licensed PS5 pads. The physics is well
    understood, which is why the sensors are cheap to make but the PS5 versions
    are not.
  </p>

  <p>
    One trade-off shows up in practice. Hall effect sticks tend to report a touch
    more linear motion, which can feel slightly different in fast FPS aim. I
    noticed it in the first session and forgot about it by the second. If you
    play competitive shooters daily, give yourself a day to adjust before you
    judge the feel.
  </p>

  <h2>Why hall effect PS5 controllers cost more</h2>

  <p>
    Sony controls the PS5 wireless protocol and licenses it to third parties. A
    pad has to pay for that license, then add the more expensive hall effect
    sensors on top. The result is a gap between what PC players pay and what PS5
    players pay. A hall effect PC pad like the GameSir G7 SE costs around $45.
    The cheapest hall effect PS5 controllers start near $180. You are paying for
    the license, the wireless chip, and the fact that Sony only authorizes a
    handful of these. That is why the list below is short.
  </p>

  <p>
    There is a budget workaround. If you are comfortable with a soldering iron,
    Gulikit sells hall effect and TMR replacement stick modules for the
    DualSense. A pair costs roughly $15 to $25, and it brings a dead stock pad
    back to life. The install is not beginner-friendly and it voids your
    warranty, but it is the cheapest path to drift-free sticks if you already
    own a pad.
  </p>

  <h2>The best hall effect PS5 controllers right now</h2>

  <p>
    Every pad here is officially licensed and ships with anti-drift magnetic
    sticks. Prices are accurate at the time of writing and move with sales, so
    treat them as a range.
  </p>

  <table class="spec-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Controller</th>
        <th>Stick tech</th>
        <th>Connection</th>
        <th>Price</th>
        <th>Best for</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>NACON Revolution 5 Pro</td>
        <td>Hall effect (sticks and triggers)</td>
        <td>Wireless</td>
        <td>$199.99</td>
        <td>Most complete anti-drift coverage</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Hexgaming Ultimate</td>
        <td>Hall effect</td>
        <td>Wireless</td>
        <td>$179.99</td>
        <td>Stock DualSense feel with hall sticks</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Razer Raiju V3 Pro</td>
        <td>Anti-drift magnetic (TMR)</td>
        <td>Wireless</td>
        <td>$179.99</td>
        <td>Competitive speed and back paddles</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Victrix Pro BFG Reloaded</td>
        <td>Hall effect (modular)</td>
        <td>Wireless</td>
        <td>$199.99</td>
        <td>Modular layout and fighting games</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>HYPR custom controller</td>
        <td>Hall effect</td>
        <td>Wireless</td>
        <td>$199 to $249</td>
        <td>Full customization</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>NACON Revolution 5 Pro</h3>

  <p>
    The NACON Revolution 5 Pro is the most complete hall effect PS5 controller
    you can buy. Both sticks and both triggers use hall effect sensors, so the
    two parts that wear out fastest on a normal pad are covered. It works on
    PS5, PS4, and PC, and it is officially licensed by Sony. The companion app
    lets you remap every button, tune dead zones, and save profiles per game.
    It lists at $199.99. The main trade-off is size. It is a large pad, and
    players with smaller hands should try it in person first.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0CLBD543P" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the NACON Revolution 5 Pro
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3>Hexgaming Ultimate</h3>

  <p>
    Hexgaming takes a stock DualSense and rebuilds it with hall effect sticks,
    extra rear buttons, and optional trigger stops. It keeps the PlayStation
    shape and layout most players already know, which matters if you have used
    a DualSense for years and do not want to relearn a new grip. Prices start
    around $179 and climb past $200 with custom finishes. If you want the
    DualSense feel with sticks that will not drift, this is the closest thing.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0F5VTDQL6" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the Hexgaming Ultimate
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3>Razer Raiju V3 Pro</h3>

  <p>
    Razer's PS5 pad uses anti-drift magnetic stick modules, four back paddles,
    and mechanical face buttons. It is tuned for competitive play, with a
    faster response feel and a shorter trigger throw. It runs $179.99. The catch
    is that it does not carry over the adaptive trigger feedback from the stock
    DualSense, which story-driven players notice more than shooter players do.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0FNDJZ3YG" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the Razer Raiju V3 Pro
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3>Victrix Pro BFG Reloaded</h3>

  <p>
    The Victrix Pro BFG is the most modular pad on this list. You can swap the
    left module, right module, and fight pad to change the layout, and hall
    effect stick modules are available. It targets fighting game players first,
    but the modular design works for anyone who wants one pad that can change
    shape. It sells for $199.99, with extra modules sold separately.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://www.pdp.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Check price on the Victrix Pro BFG
    </a>
  </p>

  <h3>HYPR custom controllers</h3>

  <p>
    HYPR builds custom PS5 controllers to order with hall effect sticks, rear
    buttons, and cosmetic choices. Prices run $199 to $249 depending on options.
    Lead time is longer than a stock pad, but you get the exact layout and look
    you want. This is the pick if you were going to customize a DualSense
    anyway and want anti-drift sticks from the start.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a class="buy-link" href="https://hyprcontrollers.com/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener sponsored">
      Build a HYPR hall effect controller
    </a>
  </p>

  <h2>TMR sticks are a step past hall effect</h2>

  <p>
    TMR stands for tunneling magnetoresistance. It reads the same kind of magnet
    a hall effect stick uses, but with lower power draw and a more linear
    output. In practice the two feel nearly identical, and both solve drift. If
    a controller lists TMR sticks, treat it as a hall effect pad with a small
    efficiency bump, not a different category. Gulikit was among the first to
    push TMR into consumer sticks, and its modules now show up inside several
    third-party pads.
  </p>

  <h2>How to pick the right one for your budget</h2>

  <p>
    Spend around $180 to $200 and you get the full feature set from any of the
    pads above. The decision comes down to three questions. Want to keep the
    DualSense shape? Pick Hexgaming. Want the most complete anti-drift coverage
    plus PC support? Pick NACON. Want competitive speed and back paddles? Pick
    Razer.
  </p>

  <p>
    If $200 is too much, the honest answer is the DIY route. A Gulikit stick
    module kit plus an afternoon of soldering gets you drift-free sticks for
    under $30. That is real money saved if you already own a DualSense and you
    are willing to open it up.
  </p>

  <h2>Who should skip a hall effect controller</h2>

  <p>
    This upgrade does not make sense for everyone. If you only play a few hours
    a month, your stock DualSense will last years, and a $200 replacement is
    wasted money. If you rely on the adaptive triggers and haptics in games like
    Astro Bot or Returnal, most third-party hall effect pads will not reproduce
    those effects, and you will miss them. If you are on a strict budget and do
    not want to solder, a standard replacement under warranty is the better
    call. The anti-drift upgrade earns its price when you play often and you are
    tired of buying pads.
  </p>

  <h2>Bottom line</h2>

  <p>
    Hall effect sticks fix the one problem that has dogged PlayStation
    controllers for a generation. On PS5 the options are limited and priced
    around $180 to $250 because of Sony's licensing. The NACON Revolution 5 Pro
    is the best all-rounder I tested. Hexgaming is the choice if you want to
    keep the DualSense feel. And if you have steady hands, a $25 Gulikit module
    kit gets you there for a fraction of the price.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>

<style>
  .spec-table {
    width: 100%;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }
  .spec-table th,
  .spec-table td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border);
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .spec-table th {
    background: var(--color-surface);
    font-size: 0.74rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    color: var(--color-text-muted);
  }
  .buy-link {
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
